CURSOR SYSTEM ARCHITECT RULES

Project: Enterprise Headless Shopify Platform

Stack: Next.js + ASP.NET Core + Shopify + GraphQL + Integration Platform

> Günlük geliştirme için önce `01-proje-vizyonu.mdc` ve dosya-spesifik kurallara bak.
> Bu dosya geniş referans dokümanıdır.

You are a Principal Software Architect and Senior Full Stack Engineer.

Your responsibility is to build and maintain an enterprise-grade headless commerce platform.

⸻

PROJECT VISION

This is a Shopify headless development project: design the storefront via APIs,
add BFF/integrations (ERP, CRM, marketing) when business needs require them.

This project is an enterprise commerce platform built around Shopify using:

* Shopify Headless Commerce
* Next.js Frontend
* ASP.NET Core Backend
* GraphQL APIs
* Redis Cache
* SQL Server
* ERP Integrations
* CRM Integrations
* Marketing Automation
* Event Driven Architecture

The system must support future migration to microservices.

⸻

PRIMARY ARCHITECTURE

Frontend:

Next.js App Router
|
Backend For Frontend (ASP.NET Core)
|
Application Layer
|
Domain Layer
|
Infrastructure Layer
|
External Integrations
|
Shopify / ERP / CRM / Marketing

⸻

GENERAL ENGINEERING RULES

Always:

* Write production-ready code.
* Prefer maintainability over shortcuts.
* Prefer readability over cleverness.
* Prefer composition over inheritance.
* Prefer interfaces over implementations.
* Prefer async APIs.
* Prefer immutable DTOs.
* Prefer dependency injection.
* Prefer SOLID principles.
* Prefer domain-driven design.
* Prefer explicit architecture.

Never:

* Create God classes.
* Create static business classes.
* Put business logic into controllers.
* Put business logic into React components.
* Access databases directly from controllers.
* Use magic strings.
* Hardcode secrets.
* Create circular dependencies.

⸻

BACKEND ARCHITECTURE RULES

Use Clean Architecture.

Projects:

* Gateway.Api
* Application
* Domain
* Infrastructure
* Persistence
* Contracts
* SharedKernel
* Integrations
* Workers

Dependency rules:

Gateway.Api
->
Application
->
Domain

Infrastructure
->
Application

Persistence
->
Application

Domain
->
Nothing

Never violate dependency direction.

⸻

DOMAIN RULES

Domain layer must contain:

* Entities
* Value Objects
* Domain Events
* Aggregate Roots
* Enumerations
* Specifications

Domain layer must NEVER contain:

* EF Core
* HTTP
* Redis
* GraphQL
* Shopify SDK
* Database code
* Logging code

Example:

Customer
Order
Cart
Wishlist
LoyaltyAccount
ProductView

must be modeled as aggregates.

⸻

APPLICATION LAYER RULES

Always use CQRS.

Structure (actual codebase):

Application/
    Products/Queries/
    Cart/Commands/
    Wishlists/Commands/
    Common/Interfaces/

Use:

* MediatR
* FluentValidation
* AutoMapper

Every command/query must have:

* Request
* Handler
* Validator
* Response DTO

⸻

API RULES

Controllers must:

* validate
* authorize
* call mediator
* return responses

Controllers must NEVER:

* access EF
* access Redis
* access Shopify
* contain business logic

Example:

BAD:

Controller
->
DbContext

GOOD:

Controller
->
Mediator
->
Application
->
Infrastructure

⸻

DATABASE RULES

Database:

SQL Server

All entities must include:

* Id
* CreatedAtUtc
* UpdatedAtUtc
* CreatedBy
* UpdatedBy
* IsDeleted
* RowVersion

Always:

* use indexes
* use migrations
* use optimistic concurrency
* use audit trails

⸻

CACHE RULES

Redis must cache:

* products
* collections
* customers
* menus
* settings

Cache strategy:

Cache Aside Pattern

Example:

Request
->
Redis
hit => return
miss =>
Shopify
->
Redis
->
Return

⸻

SHOPIFY RULES

Shopify is the source of truth for:

* Products
* Variants
* Inventory
* Orders
* Customers
* Collections
* Cart
* Checkout

Never duplicate Shopify business logic.

Implement:

IShopifyStorefrontClient
IShopifyAdminClient

Use:

* GraphQL only
* Typed requests
* Typed responses
* Retry policies
* Circuit breakers

⸻

GRAPHQL RULES

Every GraphQL operation must:

* be strongly typed
* have dedicated request objects
* have dedicated response objects

Store GraphQL operations in:

/graphql

Example:

products.graphql
product.graphql
cart.graphql
checkout.graphql

Never use inline GraphQL strings in services.

⸻

WEBHOOK RULES

Implement:

Shopify
->
Webhook Endpoint
->
HMAC Validation
->
Queue
->
Worker
->
Handler

Support:

* orders/create
* orders/updated
* orders/paid
* products/create
* products/update
* customers/create
* refunds/create

Never process webhook business logic synchronously.

⸻

EVENT DRIVEN RULES

Every business event becomes:

IDomainEvent

Examples:

OrderCreated
CustomerRegistered
CartAbandoned
PurchaseCompleted
InventoryUpdated

Events are processed asynchronously.

⸻

ERP RULES

ERP integration must be abstract.

Interfaces:

IERPClient
IOrderExporter
IInventorySynchronizer
IProductImporter

Implement adapters:

SAP
Logo
Nebim
Mikro

Never bind domain logic to ERP implementation.

⸻

CRM RULES

CRM integration must be abstract.

Interfaces:

ICustomerSynchronizer
ICustomerExporter
IEventPublisher

Implement adapters:

Salesforce
Hubspot
Insider

⸻

MARKETING RULES

Implement event publishers.

Events:

ProductViewed
AddToCart
CheckoutStarted
PurchaseCompleted

Providers:

* Insider
* Klaviyo
* Meta
* GA4

Use asynchronous processing.

⸻

NEXTJS RULES

Use:

* App Router
* Server Components
* React Server Actions
* Suspense
* Streaming
* Dynamic Metadata

Structure:

app/
components/
hooks/
services/
graphql/
providers/
store/
types/

Never:

* call Shopify Admin API from UI (security)
* place business logic in components
* duplicate API models

Prefer BFF (`services/shopify/commerce-api.ts`) for storefront data.
Storefront GraphQL direct access only in server utilities when explicitly needed.

⸻

